WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.816
Context rot: why more context is not free.

2
00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:06.531
The Anthropic post uses two phrases you will hear often.

3
00:00:06.811 --> 00:00:13.936
Context rot: as the number of tokens grows, the model’s ability to recall information from the context declines.

4
00:00:14.216 --> 00:00:19.101
Attention budget: every token added draws down a finite amount of attention.

5
00:00:19.381 --> 00:00:31.968
Their explanation is architectural (in a transformer, every token attends to every other, so relationships grow with the square of the length) plus a training one (models see fewer very long sequences).

6
00:00:32.248 --> 00:00:35.192
They describe the decline as gradual, not a cliff.

7
00:00:35.472 --> 00:00:44.432
The best-known measurement is Chroma’s July 2025 report “Context Rot,” which tested 18 models from several providers.

8
00:00:44.712 --> 00:00:48.595
On deliberately simple tasks, performance fell as input length grew.

9
00:00:48.875 --> 00:00:56.832
Distractors (text similar to the answer but wrong) made it worse, and so did lower similarity between the question and the answer text.

10
00:00:57.112 --> 00:01:09.464
On a long-conversation memory benchmark, models did much better with a focused prompt of a few hundred relevant tokens than with the full history of about 113,000 tokens.

11
00:01:09.744 --> 00:01:22.245
Earlier, the 2023 “Lost in the Middle” paper (Liu and colleagues) found that models used information at the start and end of a long context more reliably than information in the middle.

12
00:01:22.525 --> 00:01:24.104
Read the evidence carefully.

13
00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:30.400
Chroma is a vector database company, the report is not peer reviewed, and the tasks are synthetic.

14
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:35.117
Models change, and some newer ones handle long inputs better than the ones tested.

15
00:01:35.397 --> 00:01:49.349
But the direction is consistent across independent studies and with Anthropic’s own guidance, and it predicts what you will see in agent traces: an agent that has read thirty documents starts missing a fact it read in the fifth.

16
00:01:49.629 --> 00:01:52.595
The practical rule is not “keep contexts tiny.

17
00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:56.587
” It is “keep contexts relevant,” and measure on your own tasks.
